zig: a round's draws and commits in one call each

With several streams, each round of the GLM lanes verified all windows in
one forward and then did the rest one window at a time: a packed sampler
call per window (each a gather and a host readback), and a commit per
window (a commit table staged, a replay when the window was cut short, a
conv shift, and the drafter's tap pass with its blocking check). With eight
streams that is eight readbacks and up to eight tap checks a round.

- cuda_engine: `sampleWindows` draws every window in one samplePacked call
  (the sampler already takes up to eight parts: one gather and one readback
  for greedy and top-k draws; nucleus draws add their second one, and a
  retry, as before; TF_GLM_GREEDY_CHECK=1's diagnostic still reads back
  per part). Structured output's bits get a buffer a window, so the
  windows' masks can be uploaded back to back before the one draw;
  `commitWindows` commits several windows with one commit table and one
  replay for the windows cut short, one table and one conv shift for all,
  and one tap pass for the drafter (`Multi.commitTaps`, one blocking check
  per tap batch where addTaps checks every window's pass; past 64 kept rows
  it falls back to a tap pass per window).
- cuda_dflash: `commitTaps` absorbs the kept rows of any windows of a round
  in one tap pass, each window at its own row of the plane
  (commitDraftsStrided takes the whole round in order).
- cuda_lanes: verifyFn draws all windows at once; keepFn checks every
  window before it touches any (a refused batch leaves them all pending),
  then keepFn and the drafts' pre-draft commits go through `commitAll`:
  commitWindows when the engine has it, the per-window calls otherwise
  (one batch per callback; a round with drafting and non-drafting streams
  has one before the drafts and one after). The Toy test engine gets the
  batched forms, counted, so the eight-streams test pins the batched path.

The rows, tables, positions and parities are those of the per-window path;
the commit tables and the tap pass are shared within each batch (one per
nonempty callback) instead of staged per window, while each window's
position still advances on its own; the sampler's draws keep their per-row
positions. The drafter's tap pass runs over the round's kept
rows together (a wider row bucket than one window's), so its result is
checked live: on a TP=2 pair (SPLIT=1, COMM=roce, cache off), the replies
on the 56-request main set (eight at once) and the 428-case sweep are
identical to the per-window engine's and to the Python engine's. Eight
requests at once: 90.5 tok/s with drafts (89.1 before), 75.1 without
(70.6 before); one request: unchanged within 2 %.

